WEBVTT

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.002
Every pixel looks along a ray through the
scene.

00:00:03.002 --> 00:00:07.477
A neural radiance field describes that
scene with two functions of position.

00:00:07.477 --> 00:00:08.402
A density:

00:00:08.402 --> 00:00:11.202
how much light is absorbed per unit
length.

00:00:11.202 --> 00:00:14.064
Fog is thin, and a wall is dense.

00:00:14.064 --> 00:00:17.114
And a colour, the light sent out in that
direction.

00:00:17.114 --> 00:00:19.602
Nothing about surfaces is assumed.

00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:22.465
A pixel is computed by looking through the
field.

00:00:22.465 --> 00:00:27.090
Light from far away reaches the camera
only if nothing in front absorbs it.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.040
The fraction that survives is the
transmittance.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.553
It falls exponentially with the density it
has passed through.

00:00:33.553 --> 00:00:39.065
A slab of density zero point five and
thickness two lets through thirty-seven
percent.

00:00:39.065 --> 00:00:43.359
The pixel adds up, along the ray, what
survives times what is emitted,

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.691
plus whatever background gets through.

00:00:45.629 --> 00:00:47.979
Discretise the ray into intervals.

00:00:47.979 --> 00:00:52.851
Here is a clear sample, then a half-opaque
red, then a three-quarter opaque blue,

00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:54.717
in front of a white background.

00:00:54.716 --> 00:00:56.766
How much of the pixel comes from red?

00:00:56.766 --> 00:00:58.066
How much from blue?

00:00:58.066 --> 00:01:00.029
Pause and work out the shares.

00:01:01.497 --> 00:01:04.022
Blue only receives what red lets through.

00:01:04.022 --> 00:01:05.685
Red takes one half.

00:01:05.685 --> 00:01:10.172
Blue takes a half of three quarters, which
is zero point three seven five.

00:01:10.172 --> 00:01:12.160
White gets the remaining eighth.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.285
The pixel is a weighted average:

00:01:14.285 --> 00:01:15.222
a purple.

00:01:15.222 --> 00:01:17.535
The same sums give the gradients.

00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:21.235
A sample hidden behind something opaque
has weight near zero,

00:01:21.236 --> 00:01:23.298
so it receives almost no gradient.

00:01:23.255 --> 00:01:27.011
Fed raw coordinates, a network learns
smooth functions first,

00:01:27.012 --> 00:01:29.044
and fine detail extremely slowly.

00:01:29.043 --> 00:01:31.805
Here's a small network fitting one row of
a photo.

00:01:31.805 --> 00:01:32.856
It's blurry.

00:01:32.856 --> 00:01:37.930
The fix is to feed each coordinate through
sines and cosines at doubling frequencies.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.110
Nearby positions then differ a lot in the
fast features,

00:01:41.111 --> 00:01:44.007
and the same network can suddenly follow
the edges.

00:01:44.005 --> 00:01:48.831
But push the frequencies too far, and on
pixels it never saw, it does worse.

00:01:48.831 --> 00:01:50.531
That's aliasing again.
